# UNLOCK — RTL & i18n

UNLOCK is:

* Hebrew-first;
* RTL-first;
* `he-IL` by default;
* mobile-first.

This rule contains compact implementation guardrails.

Canonical product behavior lives in:

`docs/PRODUCT.md`

---

## 1. Default Product Locale

Default learner-facing language:

```text
Hebrew
```

Default direction:

```text
RTL
```

Default locale:

```text
he-IL
```

Do not treat Hebrew/RTL as optional polish to be added after implementation.

---

## 2. Technical Identifiers

Keep technical identifiers in English.

Examples:

* variables;
* functions;
* types;
* database fields;
* API names;
* route names;
* logs;
* technical file names.

Do not translate technical identifiers merely because the UI is Hebrew.

---

## 3. User-Facing Copy

Prefer the existing messages/localization layer for reusable product copy.

Current relevant area includes:

`src/messages/`

Do not scatter repeated hard-coded Hebrew strings across many components when a shared message already exists or clearly belongs in the localization layer.

Small local labels may remain local when introducing abstraction would add no value.

Do not build a large i18n framework speculatively.

---

## 4. RTL Layout

Use RTL-aware layout rather than manually mirroring every component.

Where possible, prefer logical layout concepts over physical left/right assumptions.

Be careful with:

* flex/grid alignment;
* margins/padding;
* text alignment;
* directional icons;
* back/forward controls;
* progress direction;
* mixed-direction content.

Do not assume RTL means every visual element should be reversed.

---

## 5. Mixed Hebrew / English Content

UNLOCK may display mixed content such as:

* Hebrew explanations;
* English technical terms;
* formulas;
* numbers;
* URLs;
* Question content;
* code or identifiers.

Preserve readability when bidirectional text is mixed.

Use explicit direction handling only where normal browser bidi behavior is insufficient.

---

## 6. Locale-Aware Formatting

Use locale-aware formatting where user-facing values require it.

Examples may include:

* dates;
* times;
* numbers;
* percentages.

Prefer `he-IL` behavior for Hebrew product surfaces unless the accepted feature explicitly requires another locale.

Do not hand-build formatting that the platform/runtime can handle reliably.

---

## 7. Forms and Interactive Controls

For Hebrew/RTL UI, verify the relevant interaction still feels natural.

Pay particular attention to:

* inputs;
* labels;
* validation messages;
* quiz options;
* buttons;
* navigation;
* modals;
* tables;
* directional controls.

Do not encode semantic order based only on visual left/right position.

---

## 8. Icons

Directional icons require semantic judgment.

Examples:

* back/forward arrows may need RTL-aware direction;
* play, download, close, search, check, warning, and similar semantic icons usually should not be mirrored merely because the page is RTL.

Prefer meaning over mechanical mirroring.

---

## 9. Data / Domain Boundary

Localization belongs primarily at the presentation boundary.

Do not translate canonical:

* domain enum values;
* database values;
* API identifiers;
* persisted technical keys;

for display purposes.

Map canonical values to localized presentation copy instead.

---

## 10. Accessibility

RTL/localization changes should preserve relevant accessibility behavior.

Where applicable, keep:

* semantic labels;
* logical reading order;
* keyboard navigation;
* focus behavior;
* understandable validation/error text.

Visual RTL correctness alone is not sufficient.

---

## 11. Verification

Verify RTL/i18n behavior proportionally to the UI actually changed.

Do not use this rule as a fixed testing checklist.

Testing philosophy lives in:

`docs/TESTING.md`

Use browser/E2E verification when the risk genuinely depends on rendered RTL, interaction, or browser behavior.

---

## 12. Scope Discipline

Do not use ordinary UI work to:

* redesign the localization architecture;
* add unnecessary translation infrastructure;
* convert technical identifiers to Hebrew;
* introduce multilingual product scope that V1 does not require.

Preserve i18n readiness without overbuilding it.

---

## 13. Core Principle

> Hebrew and RTL are product defaults.

> Keep technical contracts canonical and English.

> Localize at the presentation boundary.

> Prefer semantic RTL behavior over mechanical mirroring.
